**CONTROL FLOW – INTRO & IF**

// =============================
//* 1️⃣ What is Control Flow?
// =============================
// Control Flow decides which part of code will run.
// It helps programs make decisions based on conditions.

Example:
if (condition) {
  // code runs only if condition is true
}

// =============================
//* 2️⃣ IF Statement
// =============================
int age = 18;

if (age >= 18) {
  print("You are eligible to vote.");
}

int age = 20;

if (age >= 18) {
  print("You are eligible to vote.");
}

Explanation:
👉 The if statement checks the condition (age >= 18) — if true, it runs the code inside {}.

Example 2: if with a false condition

int marks = 30;

if (marks > 40) {
  print("You passed!");
}

print("Exam finished!");

Output:
Exam finished!
Because marks > 40 is false, the code inside if doesn’t run.

// Output: You are eligible to vote.

**IF-ELSE STATEMENT**

Definition:
When you want to do one thing if a condition is true, and something else if false.

if (condition) {
  // runs if true
} else {
  // runs if false
}

int age = 16;

if (age >= 18) {
  print("You can vote.");
} else {
  print("You are too young to vote.");
}


int marks = 80;

if (marks >= 50) {
  print("Passed");
} else {
  print("Failed");
}


Example:
int marks = 40;
if (marks >= 35) {
  print("Pass");
} else {
  print("Fail");
}


**IF-ELSE-IF LADDER**

Used when multiple conditions exist.

Used when you have multiple conditions to check — one by one.

Example:
int marks = 85;
if (marks >= 90) {
  print("Grade A+");
} else if (marks >= 75) {
  print("Grade A");
} else if (marks >= 60) {
  print("Grade B");
} else {
  print("Fail");
}

int temperature = 25;

if (temperature > 35) {
  print("It's hot!");
} else if (temperature > 20) {
  print("It's pleasant!");
} else {
  print("It's cold!");
}



// 🔵 EP06.4 – Switch Case Basics in Dart

**SWITCH CASE**

Used when you have multiple fixed options to check.

Example:
int day = 3;

switch (day) {
  case 1:
    print("Monday");
    break;
  case 2:
    print("Tuesday");
    break;
  case 3:
    print("Wednesday");
    break;
  default:
    print("Invalid day");
}


// 🟣 EP06.5 – Switch Case with Strings in Dart

**SWITCH WITH STRINGS**

Dart supports string values in switch.

Example:
String role = "admin";

switch (role) {
  case "admin":
    print("Welcome, Admin!");
    break;
  case "user":
    print("Welcome, User!");
    break;
  default:
    print("Unknown role");
}

// 🔴 EP06.6 – Switch Case with Enums in Dart

**SWITCH WITH ENUMS**

Enums represent fixed constant values.

enum Role { admin, user, guest }

void main() {
  Role role = Role.admin;

  switch (role) {
    case Role.admin:
      print("Admin Access");
      break;
    case Role.user:
      print("User Access");
      break;
    case Role.guest:
      print("Guest Access");
      break;
  }
}

// 🟤 EP06.7 – Ternary Operator in Dart

**TERNARY OPERATOR**

Shortcut for simple if-else.

Syntax:
(condition) ? valueIfTrue : valueIfFalse;

Example:
int age = 20;
String result = (age >= 18) ? "Adult" : "Minor";
print(result); // Adult


// ⚫ EP06.8 – Control Flow Project in Dart

**CONTROL FLOW MINI PROJECT**

Goal: Use all concepts learned (if, else-if, switch, ternary)

Example: Grade Calculator

int marks = 85;

if (marks >= 90) {
  print("A+");
} else if (marks >= 75) {
  print("A");
} else if (marks >= 60) {
  print("B");
} else {
  print("Fail");
}

// Also using ternary
String result = (marks >= 35) ? "Pass" : "Fail";
print("Result: $result");


// Build "Simple ATM Menu" using switch and if-else
// Options: Check Balance, Deposit, Withdraw, Exit


int balance = 1000;
String choice = "deposit";

switch (choice) {
  case "check":
    print("Balance: $balance");
    break;
  case "deposit":
    balance += 500;
    print("Deposited! New balance: $balance");
    break;
  case "withdraw":
    balance -= 200;
    print("Withdrawn! New balance: $balance");
    break;
  default:
    print("Invalid Option");
}


